ot cause of the two failed fast methods: one has an incorrect batch cost calculation formula, and the other's wrong batch cost leads to incorrect final values. I also found the canonical method's two core steps share the same Sinkhorn solver with consistent parameters, and v10's idea of obtaining batch cost and potentials from a single solve is reasonable, with issues lying in implementation details.\n\nI've confirmed both valid implementations of v10's idea are feasible, and the regularization parameters of the two core steps are consistent. The potential mismatch between v10's implementation and the canonical version may be caused by algorithmic differences.\n\nI've identified additional potential implementation differences between the two methods, and adjusted the implementation approach to derive required values from POT directly.\n\nAfter checking the conventions of Sinkhorn algorithms and POT's return values, I confirmed that `ot.sinkhorn` directly returns the coupling matrix P instead of potentials.\n\nI confirmed that potentials can be derived from the coupling matrix returned by `ot.sinkhorn`, and there are other relevant Sinkhorn API functions with different return values. The current implementation has two core bottlenecks: repeated Sinkhorn computations on large matrices and custom cost calculations for GeomLoss. I will explore a solution to replace both with a single fast solve.\n\nI will first check the current v15 method in the research history directory.\n"}}
